## D048 — Harness desktop tools, project folder, approvals and proposals

Decided in C21 (implementer decision for the C21 milestones, recorded for
review).

**Desktop tools.** The engine is given one dynamic-tool namespace,
`airs_desktop`, and nothing else of the desktop. Its tools are typed:
- `inventory`, `detail`: the Runtime Security inventories C08 reads.
  The DLP catalog is not offered.
- `session`, `search`: C11's investigation reads. Session content is
  already stripped by the management layer.
- `redTeam`, `supplyChain`: C13's observation reads.
- `scan`: one C03 scan with a profile named by the model, through the
  tenant's own scan key.
- `cases`: the C12 library's names and expectations.
- `proposeChange`: stages a change for the operator to review (below).

Every call goes through the same admission as the operator's own reads:
`admit_management`, `admit_scan` and the library. The call runs under the
conversation's context and is refused once that context is stale. The
desktop holds the credentials. Results are JSON:
- JSON carried as text is opened first: at most 4 layers deep and 4 MiB.
  Text still encoded past those bounds is replaced by a note, never
  passed on unchecked (P06, C21 reviews F2 and R2-F2);
- field names that carry credentials are removed at any depth (14
  names, case- and dash-insensitive);
- the conversation's key is redacted;
- a longer result becomes `{"cut": note, "partial": text}`, shortened
  until the whole envelope, escaping included, fits 24 KiB (P06, C21
  review R2-F4).

A call is parsed into a closed set:
- `deny_unknown_fields`;
- arguments of at most 40 KiB, pages of at most 50 and scan text of at
  most 16 KiB;
- any credential-like argument name is refused.

The desktop runs a call only when it belongs to the turn being answered,
and only once per call ID; a repeat is a protocol failure. Anything else
is answered "This tool or its arguments are unavailable." and nothing
runs. At most 8 calls or approvals wait at a time. A call has 60 s, and
the engine's pending tool calls are dropped with the turn (interrupt or
end). Each call carries its run and turn lease. When the turn ends, the
call is cancelled, and so is the runtime operation it admitted (P06, C21
reviews F1 and R2-F1): queued work settles NotSent and sends nothing;
work already dispatched keeps its own outcome; no result is sent late. This follows C19's rule that a reply to a request the engine forgot
is fatal.

**Proposals, never Apply.** `proposeChange` accepts Runtime and DLP kinds
only, with create, update or delete. Gateway changes need the operator's
own workspace review (C17). Before staging, the proposal passes the same
static checks a review makes, through the tenant's management client
## D049 — Workspace MCP servers for the Harness: catalog, desktop OAuth, grants and approval

Decided in C22 (implementer decision for the C22 milestones, recorded for
review).

**Catalog.** The chosen Gateway workspace's MCP servers are read through
the tenant's management client, as one more scoped Gateway read:
- `GET /mcp-servers?workspace_id`, zero-based pages of 100, at most five.
- Only the workspace's own servers are kept.
- A server's URL must be HTTPS with a host and no credentials, query or
  fragment. It is kept as the `url` crate canonicalizes it, which is what
  the engine stores.
- Repeated IDs are dropped, and the upstream (`mcp_integration_url`) is
  never kept.

A server's revision is SHA-256 over a versioned domain and the
length-prefixed ID, slug, URL, status and update time. Its engine namespace
is derived from its slug, as v1 derived it. A shared or reserved name gets a
suffix from the server's ID hash, wide enough to tell the group apart, so
the result does not depend on catalog order. An ID the engine cannot take
is shown and never granted.

**Sign-in is the desktop's, never the engine's.**
- The server must answer an unauthenticated `initialize` with `401` and a
  `resource_metadata` challenge on its own origin.
- The protected-resource metadata (RFC 9728) must name exactly the URL.
- Its authorization server must be on the same HTTPS origin, and that
  server's metadata (RFC 8414) must name itself. It must offer the code
  flow with S256 and a public client (`none`) and client registration, and
  keep every endpoint on that origin.
- A client is registered for each attempt (RFC 7591).
- The browser goes to authorize with:
  - PKCE S256 and a 256-bit `state`;
  - `resource` set to the URL (RFC 8707);
  - the shared one-attempt `127.0.0.1` loopback (D047);
  - only `mcp:servers:read`, `mcp:tools:list` and `mcp:tools:call`, as
    offered (never a wildcard).
- The code is exchanged, and later refreshed, with `resource`.
- Replies are at most 64 KiB and redirects are refused.
- An access token must be a bearer of 16-16384 visible ASCII bytes, as the
  engine requires.
- These requests are written by hand over the app's own client, like
  D047's: `oauth2`/`openidconnect` do not cover RFC 9728/8414/7591 discovery
  and registration, and D028's reasons hold.

**Grants.** A sign-in is a grant: a new UUID bound to the server's URL and
revision at that moment. Its tokens, issue time and expiry are a Keychain
item.
- Writing it, and each rotation, follows the full credential-store
  contract. The record joins the cleanup ledger, confirmed durable, and is
  reserved as in flight before it is written. It leaves the ledger only in
  the save that names it, so a stale or failed write is never left
  untracked.
- The in-flight reservation ends under the same lock as that settlement
  (P06, C22 review F4), so cleanup can never delete a record between its
  write and the save that names it. Company sign-in (D047) uses the same
  functions.
